Q: Since you worked with Vaadin 7 on frontend and backend — explain that.

Vaadin 7 is a **Java-based server-side web framework**.
Most UI is built with **Java components**, not by hand-writing HTML/CSS/JS for every control.
Vaadin renders the UI in the browser and syncs events with the server (AJAX).

────────────────────────────────────────
1) What does Vaadin 7 use on the “frontend”?

| Technology | Purpose                                      |
| ---------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| Java       | UI components + event handling               |
| GWT        | Compiles client-side Java widgets → JS       |
| HTML       | Browser DOM structure                        |
| CSS        | Styling / themes                             |
| JavaScript | Browser execution (mostly generated/managed) |
| AJAX       | Browser ↔ server communication               |

Key idea:
You write UI in Java on the server side; Vaadin + GWT handle browser rendering/interaction.

────────────────────────────────────────
2) How frontend work looks in Vaadin 7

Button button = new Button("Submit");
button.addClickListener(event -> {
    Notification.show("Button clicked!");
});

You typically do NOT write:
<button onclick="submitForm()">Submit</button>

Vaadin:
- renders component UI in browser
- sends user actions back to Java listeners on server

Components you can mention from experience:
Buttons, TextFields, ComboBoxes, Tables/Grids, Forms, Layouts, navigation, validations, CSS theming.

────────────────────────────────────────
3) Good interview answer (speak this)

“I have worked on both frontend and backend with Vaadin 7.
On the frontend side, I used Vaadin’s Java UI component model — Buttons, TextFields, ComboBoxes, Tables/Grids, Forms,
and Layouts — plus listeners, validation, navigation, and CSS-based styling.
Vaadin 7 is mainly server-driven: UI logic is in Java, and it uses GWT under the hood so client widgets become JavaScript in the browser.
I also understand HTML/CSS/JS fundamentals through customization, but my primary strength is Java enterprise backend
and integrating Vaadin UIs with Spring Boot services, security, and DB layers.
In B-SMS we later migrated legacy Vaadin modules toward Spring Boot APIs/UI modernization.”

────────────────────────────────────────
4) If asked: Did you work with JavaScript?

Be honest:
- Primary frontend experience = Vaadin 7 Java components
- HTML/CSS via Vaadin themes/customization
- Vaadin/GWT uses JS in the browser internally
- Do **not** claim deep React/Angular/vanilla JS unless true
- Core strength = Java backend + enterprise integration

────────────────────────────────────────
5) Architecture distinction (important)

| Stack              | Style                                      |
| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------ |
| Vaadin 7           | Server-side Java UI + GWT client widgets   |
| Modern Vaadin Flow | Still Java-first UI, newer web components  |
| React / Angular    | JS/TypeScript SPA frontend                 |

Position yourself as:
**Java-based frontend with Vaadin 7**, not a pure HTML/CSS/JS SPA frontend developer.

